  "live_records": {
    "q-102943": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102943",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/ae59540093c131582cf3d7c682a093eea6cbced9d8c88975a925e5c1d71123f9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
