    "q-102949": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102949",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/e542606b09d564f450aa9c2f4e2de5bf8dc197644a094c37420ee1b881f14e1f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $2.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-102949-a",
