    "q-161732": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161732",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as",
      "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/e5371e711515feccdaab3f6b10734fb35bb59c724fc408c8b67b4b54132136ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-161732-a",
