    "q-161883": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161883",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/a32e8fd24c990a831cfee9525c6b1c035fb4ee6e09deedb3ac4c899d90ceeaac.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as",
      "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220306/assets/e2865e838521a8864ad76d72e520d4b128a8fceb8c18cb9c35ba10134419c4eb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
